# What must never be committed, and why.
#
# The first group is the reason this file matters. This is
# censorship-circumvention software, developed by testing against a real home
# network and real proxy servers, and those artefacts identify a person and a
# location. Nothing in this group is recoverable once published: a fork, a
# cache or a crawler has it within the hour.

# Everything produced by testing against real hardware and real servers.
# /local/ holds pasted proxy configurations, hardware run artefacts, captured
# baselines and the panel password used during testing.
/local/
# Proxy configurations in any shape, wherever they land.
*.vless
*.vmess
*.trojan
*.subscription
configs-local/
key.txt

# Editor and OS noise.
.idea/
*.iml
.DS_Store
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# Tooling state that belongs to a working copy rather than to the project.
.claude/
.codegraph/

# Go build output. The binary is produced by the release workflow or by the
# reader; it is not a source artefact.
/caspian
/caspian-linux-*
/dist/
*.test
*.out
coverage.txt

# Node dependency trees for the Cucumber suites. bdd/README.md says how to
# install them.
bdd/**/node_modules/

# NOTE for whoever edits this next.
#
# This file used to carry a bare "docs" rule, inherited from an Android
# project's gitignore along with .gradle, *.apk and *.jks. On a fresh clone it
# excluded the entire docs/ directory, so the repository would have published
# with every documentation link in README.md pointing at a file that was never
# committed. It was invisible because the directory had been tracked before the
# rule arrived, and gitignore does not apply to files already tracked.
#
# If you add a rule here, run: git status --porcelain --ignored
# and read what it excludes, rather than trusting the pattern to mean what you
# meant.
